course: books
lesson: 74
title: Just So Stories (Rudyard Kipling), 1: How the Whale got his Throat
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A Whale has eaten every fish in the sea except one small, clever 'Stute Fish, who hides behind the Whale's right ear. When the Whale says he is still hungry, the little fish slyly asks whether he has ever tasted Man.
explain: The Whale swims to a far spot in the ocean and swallows a shipwrecked sailor in one gulp, together with his raft, his blue trousers, his braces and his pocket-knife.
explain: Inside, the sailor jumps, bangs and dances so wildly that the Whale gets hiccups, so the Whale carries him all the way home to the white cliffs and lets him walk out.
explain: But on the way the clever sailor has cut his raft into a criss-cross grating, tied it with his braces and jammed it in the Whale's throat; ever since, whales can only swallow very, very small food.
explain: Kipling tells it out loud to a child he calls "Best Beloved", with funny made-up words, a teasing reminder about the braces, and a happy, silly ending.
fact: The Just So Stories (1902) are Rudyard Kipling's made-up stories of how animals got their shapes; he first told them to his own children, who wanted them told "just so", word for word.
fact: The Whale ate "the starfish and the garfish, and the crab and the dab", until only one small 'Stute Fish was left; "'Stute" is short for "astute", which means clever.
fact: The 'Stute Fish calls the Whale "Noble and generous Cetacean" ("Cetacean" = a member of the whale and dolphin family) and says Man is "Nice but nubbly" (nubbly = lumpy).
fact: The Fish sends him to "latitude Fifty North, longitude Forty West", a real point in the cold North Atlantic Ocean, far from any land.
fact: The sailor (the "Mariner") is "a man of infinite-resource-and-sagacity", which means he is endlessly clever and full of good sense.
fact: The Mariner sits on his raft trailing his toes in the water, because "he had his Mummy's leave to paddle"; the Whale opens his mouth "back and back and back till it nearly touched his tail", swallows him, then turns round three times on his tail.
fact: He wears blue canvas "breeches" (trousers) and "suspenders" (braces that hold trousers up), and carries a "jack-knife" (a folding pocket-knife); Kipling keeps saying "you must not forget the suspenders".
fact: Inside the Whale the Mariner "stumped and he jumped and he thumped and he bumped", and danced hornpipes (a lively sailors' dance), giving the Whale "hiccoughs", an old spelling of hiccups.
fact: The Mariner says "Take me to my natal-shore and the white-cliffs-of-Albion": "natal" = where he was born, "Albion" = an old name for Britain, whose white chalk cliffs stand at Dover.
fact: On the way the Mariner cuts the raft into "a little square grating all running criss-cross", ties it with the suspenders and drags it into the Whale's throat, where it sticks.
fact: His rhyme is "By means of a grating / I have stopped your ating"; Kipling says he was "an Hi-ber-ni-an" (an Irishman), joking that his accent made "eating" sound like "ating".
fact: The Whale shouts a train-guard's call, "Change here for Winchester, Ashuelot, Nashua, Keene", as if his mouth were a railway station, and "and stations on the Fitchburg Road"; just as he says "Fitch", the Mariner walks out onto the "shingle" (a beach of small pebbles). He takes his jack-knife home with him.
fact: The 'Stute Fish hides in the mud under the "Door-sills of the Equator", afraid the Whale might be angry with him.
fact: Real biology: the biggest whales (blue, humpback) have no teeth but baleen, rows of bristly plates in the mouth that sieve tiny shrimp-like krill and small fish out of the water, a bit like a real "grating".
fact: Baleen is made of keratin, the same stuff as our fingernails, and whales are born with it; no sailor put it there, and a blue whale's throat is far too narrow to swallow a person.
fact: Whales are mammals, not fish: they breathe air through a blowhole and feed their babies on milk.
fact: The poem after the story says you are at "Fifty North and Forty West" when a ship rolls in a storm and everyone feels seasick.
exercise: What did the Whale eat at the start of the story? | Every fish he could find in all the sea | Only the starfish and the little crabs | Seaweed, sand and the shells of oysters | Nothing at all, for he had no mouth yet
exercise: Where did the small 'Stute Fish swim to keep safe? | A little behind the Whale's right ear | Deep inside the Whale's warm, dark tummy | Right on the tip of the Whale's long tail | Down in the mud under the Door-sills
exercise: What does "'Stute" mean? | Clever | Very silly | Very tiny | Easily scared
exercise: What did the 'Stute Fish ask the hungry Whale? | Whether he had ever tasted Man | Whether he would like a big cake | Whether he could swim to England | Whether he had seen any whales
exercise: How did the Fish say Man tastes? | "Nice but nubbly" | "Sweet and salty" | "Tough and chewy" | "Rich but fishy"
exercise: Where would the Whale find the Mariner? | At latitude Fifty North, longitude Forty West | On the white cliffs at the edge of the land | Under the Door-sills of the Equator | On a beach of shingle by a railway station
exercise: What was the Mariner sitting on? | A raft in the middle of the sea | A rock beside a sandy desert island | The deck of a big sailing ship | A palm tree on a little beach
exercise: Which list matches what the Mariner had with him? | Blue canvas breeches, suspenders and a jack-knife | A red coat, a hat, a lamp and a fishing rod | A raft, an oar, a compass and a sack of bread | Boots, a map, a pipe and a bag of gold coins
exercise: What are "suspenders" in this story? | Braces that hold trousers up | A pair of thick woolly sea socks | Ropes for tying a boat | Hooks for catching fish
exercise: Why had the Mariner been trailing his toes in the water? | His mummy had given him leave to paddle | He was trying to catch fish with them | He wanted to call the Whale to come | His feet were too hot from the sun
exercise: How did the Whale swallow the Mariner? | He opened his mouth till it nearly touched his tail | He sucked him up through the blowhole on his head | He bit the raft into little pieces first | He waited till the Mariner fell asleep
exercise: What did the Mariner do inside the Whale? | Jumped, thumped, banged and danced hornpipes | Sat very quietly and waited to be let out | Lit a fire and cooked himself some fish | Slept in the warm dark until morning
exercise: What problem did the Mariner's dancing give the Whale? | Hiccoughs | A toothache | Seasickness | A sore tail
exercise: When the Whale told him to come out, where did the Mariner ask to be taken? | His natal-shore and the white-cliffs-of-Albion | The middle of the sea, latitude Fifty North | The Door-sills of the Equator in the mud | A railway station called Fitchburg Road
exercise: What is "Albion"? | An old name for Britain | A kind of white whale from the north | A sailor's dance | A big ship
exercise: What did the Mariner make from his raft? | A criss-cross grating | A little wooden boat | A ladder to climb out | A sail and a mast
exercise: What did he tie the grating with? | His suspenders | His blue breeches | Long strings of seaweed | A long fishing line
exercise: What did the Whale say as he opened his mouth on the beach? | "Change here for Winchester, Ashuelot, Nashua, Keene" | "Come out and behave yourself, I've got the hiccoughs" | "One at a time is enough, so I shall eat no more men" | "By means of a grating I have stopped your ating"
exercise: At which word did the Mariner walk out? | "Fitch" | "Ashuelot" | "Change" | "Nashua"
exercise: What does "Hibernian" mean? | Irish | Hungry | Sleepy | Clever
exercise: What could the Whale eat after that day? | Only very, very small fish | Only men, boys and little girls | Only seaweed and water plants | Nothing at all for the rest of his life
exercise: Where did the 'Stute Fish go at the end, and why? | Into the mud under the Door-sills of the Equator, afraid the Whale was angry | Back behind the Whale's right ear, because it was the safest place to hide | Home to the Mariner's mother, who had promised to keep it as a pet | To latitude Fifty North, to find another sailor for the Whale to eat
exercise: Which of the Mariner's things did he take home? | The jack-knife | His blue suspenders | What was left of the raft | The grating he had made
exercise: Real biology: what do the biggest whales really use to sieve their food? | Baleen plates in the mouth | A grating left by a sailor | Sharp teeth like a shark's | Their long, narrow throats
exercise: Real biology: what do blue whales mostly eat? | Krill, tiny shrimp-like animals | Seals and penguins on the ice | Sailors who fall into the sea | Big fish such as sharks and tuna
exercise: Real biology: are whales fish? | No, they are mammals that breathe air | Yes, they are the biggest fish of all | Yes, but only the very small ones | No, they are giant sea reptiles
test: Liam, 9: "The Whale ate the sailor and never let him out." Put him right. | The sailor walked out on the beach near his home | The sailor was rescued by a passing fishing boat | The 'Stute Fish pulled him out by his braces | The sailor cut a hole in the Whale and swam away
test: Why do you think the 'Stute Fish sent the Whale to find a man? | So the Whale would not eat him, the last fish left | Because he wanted the Mariner to be rescued | Because men are the best food in all the sea | So the Whale would get the hiccoughs on purpose
test: Kipling keeps saying "you must not forget the suspenders". Why does it matter at the end? | The Mariner used them to tie the grating | They held the Whale's mouth wide open | They were left on the raft in the sea | The Mariner gave them to the 'Stute Fish
test: Jayden, Gen Alpha: "Mariner's lowkey the GOAT, no cap, he cooked." ("lowkey" = quietly, "GOAT" = the best ever, "no cap" = for real, "he cooked" = he did brilliantly.) What did he do so well? | Got home and blocked the Whale's throat for ever | Ate up all the fishes in the sea before the Whale | Cooked a fish supper on his raft in the storm | Taught the Whale how to dance the hornpipe
test: What would have happened if the Mariner had kept quiet inside the Whale? | The Whale would have had no reason to take him home | The Whale would have spat him out at once anyway | The 'Stute Fish would have come in to rescue him | The grating would have made itself out of the raft
test: True or false: the story says whales today never eat men or boys or little girls, because of the grating. | True: that is the story's made-up reason | False: the story says they eat them every day | True: and the Mariner says so in his rhyme | False: the story never talks about children
test: Nan in Glasgow: "Och, the wee fish was a right fly one, so he was." ("wee" = small, "fly" = sly and clever.) Which fish does she mean? | The small 'Stute Fish | The garfish | The dab | The really truly twirly-whirly eel
test: Mason says: "Whales got baleen when a sailor jammed a raft in one." Put him right. | Whales are born with baleen; it grew over millions of years | It was a fisherman's net that got stuck in the first whale | Only whales that swallowed sailors grew baleen afterwards | Baleen is wood, so it must come from a raft or a boat
test: Why does the Mariner rhyme "grating" with "ating"? | Kipling jokes that he is Irish and says "eating" that way | He could not spell, because he was only a little boy | "Ating" is the old word for "eating" in a sailor's song | The Whale's hiccoughs made the word come out wrong
test: Dad, dead sarcastic: "Oh great, let's all go paddle at Fifty North." What does the poem say it's like there? | Stormy, the ship rolling and everyone seasick | Warm and calm, a lovely spot for paddling | A sandy beach with white cliffs behind it | Full of whales lining up to swallow you
test: Sophie, Texas: "Y'all, that Whale just turned round three times on his tail like a dang dog." When did he do that? | Right after he swallowed the Mariner | When he reached the white cliffs | When he first got the bad hiccoughs | When he heard the Mariner's rhyme
test: Which word best describes the Mariner? | Resourceful | Greedy and selfish | Easily frightened | Cruel to animals
test: Dylan, Kiwi: "Yeah nah, the Whale's not the bad guy, he was just hungry, eh." ("yeah nah" = no.) What did the Whale do that was greedy? | Ate every fish in the sea but one | Stole the Mariner's jack-knife | Ate the Mariner's mother's dinner | Pushed the raft far out to sea
test: ★★★ Count carefully: how many separate things did the Whale swallow in one gulp, counting the Mariner himself and each thing he had with him? | Five | Four | Three | Six
test: ★★★ The Mariner walked out wearing his breeches and took the jack-knife home. What of his things did he NOT take home? | The raft and the suspenders | The breeches and the suspenders | The jack-knife and the raft | Only the suspenders
test: ★★★ Read carefully: did the Mariner make the grating before or after he asked to be taken home? | After: he made it while the Whale was swimming | Before: he made it on the raft in the sea | Before: he made it as soon as he was swallowed | After: he made it once he stood on the shingle
exam: Identify the location to which the cetacean was directed by the small 'Stute Fish in pursuit of a human meal. | Latitude fifty north, longitude forty west | The shingle beneath the cliffs of Albion | The Door-sills of the Equator in the mud | The terminus of the Fitchburg railway road
exam: Ascertain the means by which the shipwrecked Mariner secured the grating within the throat of the Whale. | By binding it with his suspenders | By nailing it with his jack-knife | By weaving it from his breeches | By gluing it with the raft's tar
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the alimentary consequence of the grating for the Whale. | He could thereafter consume only very small fish | He could thereafter consume only shipwrecked men | He was thereafter unable to consume any water | He was obliged to consume the 'Stute Fish at once
exam: State, in accordance with established zoology, the structure by which the largest whales filter their sustenance. | Plates of baleen situated within the mouth | A wooden grating lodged within the throat | Rows of sharp teeth resembling a shark's | A sieve of bone located in the blowhole
